\label{def:commitment}
An $(n,M,\epsilon_c,\epsilon_b,\epsilon_s)$ \emph{commitment code} over $\mathcal{G}$ is a two-party interactive protocol between a committer (Alice) and a receiver (Bob) with the following phases and properties.

\textbf{1. Commit phase:}
For every commitment $m\in[M]$, the channel $\mathcal{G}$ is used $n$ times. Between any two uses of the noisy channel, the parties may exchange an arbitrary finite amount of noiseless communication. All parties may use private randomness.

We denote by the random variable $V_b$ the receiver's complete view at the end of the commit phase, including all channel outputs, all noiseless communication, and all of the receiver's private randomness. When the sender honestly commits to $m$, the resulting distribution of $V_b$ is denoted by $P_{V_b|m}$.

\textbf{2. Reveal phase and verification:}
After the commit phase, the sender reveals the committed message $m$ by providing an opening $O$ to the receiver. The receiver applies a verification rule $\mathsf{Acc}(V_b,m,O)\in\{0,1\}$, where $\mathsf{Acc}(V_b,m,O)=1$ means that the receiver accepts $m$ as a valid commitment.

\textbf{a) $\epsilon_c$-concealing code:}
For every pair of $m,m'\in[M]$,
\[
\left\|P_{V_b|m}-P_{V_b|m'}\right\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
\leq \epsilon_c.
\]

\textbf{b) $\epsilon_b$-binding code:}
For every committer strategy sending an input sequence $x^n$ through the noisy channel (not necessarily from the DI code in the dishonest case) and generating the openings $O_m$ and $O_{m'}$ for any pair of messages $m,m'\in[M]$,
\[
\Pr\left\{
    \mathsf{Acc}(V_{b},O_m|x^n)=1
    \ \cap\
    \mathsf{Acc}(V_{b},O_{m'}|x^n)=1\right\}
\leq \epsilon_b.
\]

\textbf{c) $\epsilon_s$-sound code:}
For every commitment $m\in[M]$ and honest behaviour by the sender,
\[
\Pr\left\{\mathsf{Acc}(V_b,O_m\mid m)=1
\right\}
\geq 1-\epsilon_s.
\]

It is only left to show the last claim. Directly from the concealment condition in Theorem~\ref{thm:concealment}, it is clear that, for any $\nu>0$, there exist small enough $\gamma>0$ and large enough $n$ such that 
    \begin{equation}
    \dot R_\text{BC}(n)= \dot R_\text{DI}-\nu\leq \dot R_\text{DI}(n)-\gamma -\frac{C_T(\mathcal{G})+\delta}{\log n},
    \end{equation}
    since the last term vanishes as $n$ increases. Hence, the concealment condition can be satisfied for any $\nu>0$. Finally, since $\nu$ can also be chosen arbitrarily small (without breaking the previous argument), we have that $\dot R_\text{DI}$ must be an achievable rate for commitment.

The $L$-layer capacity-achieving DI codes described in \cite{CDBW:Gauss}, with $L>0$ a constant integer, have 
\begin{equation}
\lambda_1=2L\Phi(-\log n)\quad\text{and}\quad\lambda_2=2\Phi(-\log n),
\end{equation}
which vanish as $n\to\infty$ since, by Proposition~\ref{prop:small_projection}, $\Phi(-\log n)=\exp[-\Theta((\log n)^2)]$. Hence, those codes are reliable and can achieve a linearithmic rate of $\dot R_\text{DI}=\frac12$. Then, by Theorem~\ref{thm:HBC}, $\dot R_\text{BC}=\frac12$ is also achievable. The capacity is the supremum of all achievable rates, and it is hence lower bounded by $\dot C_\text{hBC}(\mathcal{G})\geq\dot R_\text{BC}=\frac12$.

\label{thm:main}
Given a binding projection-based DI code and $\gamma$, one can construct a BC code in the dishonest setting, following the strategy in Section~\ref{sec:strategy_concealment}, which is
\[
\begin{split}
&\epsilon_c \text{-concealing with}\hspace{10pt}\epsilon_c=2^{-\frac12\gamma n\log n},\\
&\epsilon_b \text{-binding with}\hspace{23pt}\epsilon_b=2\Phi\left(-\log n\right),\\
&\epsilon_s \text{-sound with}\hspace{29pt}\epsilon_s=2\Phi\left(-\log n\right),
\end{split}
\]
achieving a linearithmic rate of $\dot R_{BC}=\frac14$. Since $\epsilon_c,\epsilon_b,\epsilon_s\to0$ as $n\to\infty$, the code is reliable.
